  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       § 3  STYLE GUIDANCE
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">03 &nbsp; Style</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Tailwind-level direction.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="body-grid">
      <div class="gutter">Voice</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The aesthetic register to aim for is closer to an FT special report or a
          McKinsey Quarterly piece than to a SaaS dashboard. That means:
          <em>serif throughout</em>, hairlines and rules instead of card chrome,
          one accent at most, monospace reserved for IDs and figures, generous
          margins, and column-grid alignment so the page reads like printed pages
          rather than a stack of widgets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- type specimens -->
    <div class="specimens">
      <div class="spec serif">
        <div class="lbl">Display &amp; body — Source Serif&nbsp;4</div>
        <div class="sample-1">Can China staff its chip-tooling push?</div>
        <div class="sample-2">AMEC reports 1,548 R&amp;D personnel — 52.24% of all employees — and discloses that 57.88% of that R&amp;D layer holds a master's or doctoral degree.</div>
        <div class="sample-3">Use weights 400 / 500 / 600. Optical sizes on.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="spec sans">
        <div class="lbl">Chrome &amp; labels — IBM Plex Sans</div>
        <div class="sample-1">Listed-firm disclosure</div>
        <div class="sample-2">Eyebrows, navigation, source badges, captions, footnotes. Tracked +0.16em uppercase for kickers, +0.04em sentence for ordinary chrome.</div>
        <div class="sample-3">11 / 12 / 14 PX  ·  UPPERCASE FOR LABELS</div>
      </div>
      <div class="spec mono">
        <div class="lbl">Figures &amp; IDs — IBM Plex Mono</div>
        <div class="sample-1">CN_FILING_AMEC_2025</div>
        <div class="sample-2">Source IDs, evidence row IDs, methodology references, numeric annotations on charts. Never for body or headlines.</div>
        <div class="sample-3">tabular-nums on for tables and stat strips</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- tokens -->
    <div class="tok-group-title" style="margin-top:36px;">Color tokens</div>
    <div class="tokens">
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--paper</div>
        <div class="desc">Page background. Warm off-white, the colour of a printed page. Avoid pure white.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#f3efe7"></span>#F3EFE7&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-50</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--paper-deep</div>
        <div class="desc">Pull-quote &amp; secondary surface. Same warmth, one step darker.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#ece7dc"></span>#ECE7DC&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-100</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--ink</div>
        <div class="desc">Primary text. Warmer than pure black; reads on paper.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#14110d"></span>#14110D&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-950</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--muted</div>
        <div class="desc">Eyebrows, captions, secondary metadata, denominators.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#6b6358"></span>#6B6358&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-500</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--rule</div>
        <div class="desc">Primary 1px hairline — section dividers, exhibit edges.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#1c1917"></span>#1C1917&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-900</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--rule-hair</div>
        <div class="desc">Tertiary hairline — list dividers, table rows.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#c8c2b6"></span>#C8C2B6&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">stone-300</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--accent</div>
        <div class="desc">Single editorial highlight. Section markers, drop caps, the one highlighted city on the map.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#b07418"></span>#B07418&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">amber-700</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--sig-green</div>
        <div class="desc">Keep — source-checked badges. The only place green appears.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#5a6b3e"></span>#5A6B3E&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">olive (custom)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">--black</div>
        <div class="desc">Reverse-out sections only (the city signal map). Not for ink.</div>
        <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#08070a"></span>#08070A&nbsp;·&nbsp;<span style="font-family:var(--sans);">zinc-950</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tok-group-title" style="margin-top:32px;">Spacing &amp; rules</div>
    <div class="tokens">
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">grid</div>
        <div class="desc">12-column, 1240px max content width, 56px outer margins desktop. Reduce to 4-column at 880px.</div>
        <div class="swatch">tw: max-w-[1240px] px-14</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">section spacing</div>
        <div class="desc">96px between sections, 36px between section-header and body, 28px between paragraphs.</div>
        <div class="swatch">mt-24 / mt-9 / mb-7</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">rules</div>
        <div class="desc">1px solid for section dividers; 1px dotted/hair for in-section dividers; no shadows, no borders &gt;1px, no rounded corners over 0px on text containers.</div>
        <div class="swatch">border-t border-stone-900</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">card chrome</div>
        <div class="desc">Remove. White cards on gray are SaaS grammar. Replace with rule-bounded regions and 0px-radius edges.</div>
        <div class="swatch">— delete —</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">shadows</div>
        <div class="desc">None on body content. Allow a single ultra-soft drop shadow only on raised exhibits (e.g. inset map overlay).</div>
        <div class="swatch">shadow-[0_24px_50px_-32px_rgba(15,12,8,.18)]</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">type sizes</div>
        <div class="desc">Display 56–76 / Section 36–40 / Body 18–19 / Captions 13–14 / Chrome 10–12. Track chrome +0.16em uppercase.</div>
        <div class="swatch">text-[56px]/[36px]/[19px]/[11px]</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tok-row">
        <div class="name">numerals</div>
        <div class="desc">Tabular numerals everywhere a column of figures appears (exhibits, stat strips, dossier values). Old-style numerals in body prose.</div>
        <div class="swatch">font-feature-settings: "tnum" / "onum"</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       § 4  WHAT TO REMOVE
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">04 &nbsp; Subtract</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">What to remove.</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="crit-list" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The <em>Core Question</em> pull-out in the hero.</h3>
          <p>It restates the headline. A pull-quote that paraphrases the headline weakens both. Put the standfirst where the pull-out is; move the exhibit there instead.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Two of the three hero CTAs.</h3>
          <p>Keep <em>Read the brief</em> as the single primary action. Demote <em>Open firm dossiers</em> and <em>Inspect source rows</em> to a quiet text link or the masthead nav.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Section 01 <em>Analyst Brief</em>'s four-card grid — or section 02 <em>Findings</em>. Not both.</h3>
          <p>They carry the same content in different formats. The proposal in §02 above merges them into a single argument scroll with prose lede and numbered subordinate findings.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>White rounded-corner cards on light-gray background.</h3>
          <p>Replace every card grid with rule-bounded regions. Cards are SaaS; rules are editorial. Keep card containers only inside the dark <em>City signal</em> exhibit, where they read as data-viz UI rather than chrome.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The per-block eyebrows.</h3>
          <p><span style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.16em;">LISTED-COMPANY DISCLOSURE</span>, <span style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.16em;">COMPARATOR FRAME</span>, <span style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.16em;">FIRM WORKFORCE CHART</span>, <span style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.16em;">PUBLIC PRODUCT RECORD</span> — the page is shouting. Keep an eyebrow only on the section opener.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The beta caveat in headline weight.</h3>
          <p>It's important; it isn't the lede. Move to a methodology line under the hero CTA in muted Plex Sans, 10–11px, tracked.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The full nav row at the very top (<em>HOME / FIRMS / EXPLORER</em>) inside the article column.</h3>
          <p>It floats unattached. Pin it to a thin masthead with the publication name, and treat it as chrome, not content.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       § 5  WHAT NOT TO CHANGE
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">05 &nbsp; Keep</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">What not to change.</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="crit-list" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The editorial restraint.</h3>
          <p>No rankings, no scores, no composite indices, no leaderboards. This is the single feature that distinguishes the product from every other China-tech tracker; protect it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The dark <em>City signal map</em> exhibit.</h3>
          <p>This is the strongest piece on the page. Reverse-out section, gridded background, restrained yellow highlight on the active node, paired with an inspector that names what the size means and what it doesn't. Use it as the visual template for any future exhibit.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The denominator discipline.</h3>
          <p>Every figure in the dossiers labels its denominator and refuses to roll up across categories. Keep that habit visible in the exhibits; it's the page's intellectual signature.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The <em>Source checked</em> badge and the source-row ID treatment.</h3>
          <p>The green check + monospace source-id pattern (<span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;">CN_FILING_AMEC_2025</span>) reads as serious archival metadata. Keep it; reuse it consistently.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The comparator frame's <em>Compare on / Do not compare on</em> list.</h3>
          <p>This is the most McKinsey-feeling piece of writing on the site. It belongs in the brief, not in a competing home section, but the framing itself is exactly right.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The bilingual firm names.</h3>
          <p><em>AMEC&nbsp;/&nbsp;中微公司</em>, <em>Piotech&nbsp;/&nbsp;拓荆科技</em>. Quiet, factual, signals primary-source contact. Keep at every dossier head.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       § 6  OTHER NOTES
       ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">06 &nbsp; Notes</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Other observations.</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="crit-list" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Give the brief a date and an issue number.</h3>
          <p>Public-source monitors gain credibility by versioning visibly. <em>Issue 04 · May 24, 2026</em> tells the reader the work is maintained on a cadence. <em>april 17 (v1)</em> in the tab title is doing this work in the wrong place.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Lead with an exhibit, not with cards.</h3>
          <p>The four anchor firms each have one disclosed figure that is interesting on its own (AMEC's 52.24% R&amp;D share, ACM's 49.42% technical-staff share, NAURA's 21,101, Piotech's 638). A four-row exhibit beside the headline does more work than the current Core Question pull-out.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Section numbers are doing two jobs. Pick one.</h3>
          <p>Right now <em>01 ANALYST BRIEF</em> reads both as a chapter marker and as an eyebrow. Promote it (§&nbsp;01 in section heads, large, with a hair-rule above) or drop it. The half-state is what reads as SaaS.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Promote the <em>discipline-to-segment matrix</em> earlier.</h3>
          <p>It is the cleverest piece of the whole atlas. Inside the home it currently sits below the dossier exhibit. Either pull it into the argument section as the second exhibit, or give it its own section header (<em>“Why STEM totals miss”</em>) and let the matrix stand alone.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>Resist visualising what the data can't carry.</h3>
          <p>The footprint grid (<em>Chinese toolmakers to watch</em>) is on the edge. <em>CORE SOURCE</em> filled cells next to <em>NO CURRENT RECORD</em> dashed cells look like a scorecard to a casual reader, even though the captions say otherwise. Consider re-rendering as a coverage matrix (dots scaled to row-count) so the visual cue matches the analytical one.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>One typeface change does most of the work.</h3>
          <p>The single highest-leverage change is moving body and headlines to an editorial serif. The page is currently set in a default neo-grotesque sans that signals web product. A serif (Source Serif&nbsp;4, Tiempos, Lyon, GT Sectra — the family doesn't matter; the register does) repositions the entire artefact toward publication.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>The dossier page header has the right idea — promote it home.</h3>
          <p>Big Latin name, smaller Hanzi, a single-line standfirst, segment chips, hairline rule, then the body in two columns (Analyst Read / Boundary Condition). That's the exact grammar the home hero should adopt.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- pull -->
  <div class="pull" style="margin-top:64px; margin-left:128px;">
    The product is doing the difficult half right: it has refused to invent a
    league table and built a record of denominators instead. The home page just
    hasn't caught up to the seriousness of the work behind it.
    <span class="src">Summary</span>
  </div>

  <footer class="colophon">
    <span><b>Atlas&nbsp;·&nbsp;Design Review</b></span>
    <span>Prepared as a single-document memo&nbsp;·&nbsp;HTML</span>
    <span>End of memo</span>
  </footer>

</article>

